# The driver this package installs is proprietary and is never redistributed
# here, so there is no upstream tarball for uscan to fetch on our behalf. What
# there IS is a place where a new driver version would appear first, and this
# file records it so the check is a command rather than somebody's memory.
#
# Broadcom publishes the driver here; Canonical packages the same version into
# the OEM pocket that install.sh prefers.
#
# Two deliberate departures from a normal watch file:
#
#   * dversionmangle rewrites this package's own version to the driver version
#     pinned in SHA256SUMS. Without it uscan compared Broadcom's 5.15.285
#     against our 0.3.0 and reported a new upstream release on every single run,
#     which is the same as reporting nothing at all. What we want to know is
#     whether Broadcom has published a driver newer than the one we pin.
#
#   * There is no uupdate. The default action would build a new source tree
#     around the downloaded tarball, and that tarball is the proprietary driver:
#     running it would put the blob inside a source package that this project
#     could then upload. Nothing here may ever do that. This file reports; a
#     human changes the pins by hand.
version=5
opts="mode=http,pgpmode=none,dversionmangle=s/.*/5.15.285.5.15.010.0/" \
  https://packages.broadcom.com/artifactory/dell-controlvault-drivers/ \
  brcm_linux_fp_([\d.]+)_([\d.]+)\.tgz debian
